 to say scanning the possibilities of  extracting energy from the sea  this was a choice that i made  many years ago  but what i  was shocked by is the speed and the  shamelessness with which  the industrial interests have thrown to  the wastebasket all the environmental  measures  that had been very laboriously taken  that was oceanographer and  environmentalist jack custo a new  documentary about his life called  becoming gusto the film looks at his  journey from an explorer of the  underwater world to his crusade to  protect it producer mirador chandra  spoke to bloomberg about the process of  making the documentary and how film can  change the world  i think that it's through storytelling  and connecting people  through these stories that we can talk  about larger issues so you know this is  a film for example about jacques eve  cousteau but it's about how we  um  are going to  experience and protect the environment  you know and that speaks to the  zeitgeist of what we're talking about  today once one of the most famous people  in the world for his tv projects  becoming castel looks to introduce him  to a whole new audience i think that  with the cops summit his message is more  important than ever because  of several reasons i mean we've sort of  forgotten his legacy and that he was  really sounding the alarm  50 years ago so to have that kind of  historic um understanding uh that it's  that we were talking about this before  climate change and before you know  talking about greenhouse gases was part  of our lexicon puts it into perspective  that we cannot wait any longer what did  you find surprising in learning more  about giacasto well i found it  surprising that he  invented the aqualung which is the  precursor to scuba  that was pretty exciting that he was  such a he was an inventor  that he was in the french navy i didn't  know about that um you know my knowledge  of him really started when he was a  television celebrity and from his tv  shows he gained 