 fires so it was  that was shocking but i think the thing  that really threw me over the edge was  on december 27 in melbourne my hometown  where i lived for 34 years it was um 47  degrees celsius  and i lived there for 34 years and it  never got above 44 degrees which was  generally one week in february in late  summer so this was early summer  and you know anecdotally that's a three  degree  temperature increase in my lifetime and  that's not the science it's one and a  half degrees that we've increased  temperature but in what is ground zero  for climate change the temperature has  actually increased three degrees in my  hometown and everyone was kind of  walking around  in that australian kind of laconic  relaxed way going yeah it's really hot  and i was kind of like guys it's like  baghdad what is happening here um so  that was one of the moments that really  affected me and then you know being in  sydney in january and the whole city was  in smoke again so i think by the time  you know we got on a plane to go home to  l.a i was kind of like i think i think  there's a film here  but i thought i always thought from the  beginning it shouldn't just be stories  about fires and victims it needed to be  about climate change and it needed to be  all-encompassing  that was ava orna the executive producer  and director of burning let's keep it on  documentaries and the way they can shed  light on the impact of climate change  bloomberg green is launching a  competition for filmmakers and one of  the judges is our own john frah and he  joins us now the obvious place to start  what was the reasoning behind a green  docks competition well storytelling is  one of the best ways to bring the  climate crisis and climate solutions i'm  home to people it's what we try to do in  bloomberg green  every day and it's what other media  outlets try to do all the time as well  but you know climate change lends itself  especially well to a visual story  storytelling um approach um and 